<div class="eyebrow">OAuth patterns</div>

# DCR and CIMD flows

This server demonstrates two ways an MCP client can become known to the
authorization server:

- **Dynamic Client Registration (DCR)** at `/oauth/register`
- **Client ID Metadata Documents (CIMD)** where the `client_id` is a hosted
  metadata URL

## DCR sequence

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Client as MCP Client
    participant AS as Authorization Server
    participant User as User/Browser
    participant MCP as Protected MCP Server

    Client->>AS: GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server
    Client->>AS: POST /register with client metadata
    AS-->>Client: client_id (+ optional client_secret)
    Client->>User: Open authorization URL with PKCE
    User->>AS: Approve consent
    AS-->>Client: Redirect with authorization code
    Client->>AS: POST /token with code + code_verifier
    AS-->>Client: access_token
    Client->>MCP: MCP request with Bearer token
    MCP-->>Client: Protected result
```

## CIMD sequence

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Client as MCP Client
    participant AS as Authorization Server
    participant Metadata as Client Metadata URL
    participant User as User/Browser
    participant MCP as Protected MCP Server

    Client->>AS: /authorize?client_id=https://client.example.com/metadata.json
    AS->>Metadata: GET client metadata document
    Metadata-->>AS: Client metadata
    AS->>AS: Validate metadata + redirect_uri + PKCE
    User->>AS: Approve consent
    AS-->>Client: Redirect with authorization code
    Client->>AS: POST /token with code + code_verifier
    AS-->>Client: access_token
    Client->>MCP: MCP request with Bearer token
    MCP-->>Client: Protected result
```

## Comparison

| Concern | DCR | CIMD |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Client identity | Server-issued `client_id` | URL-valued `client_id` |
| Initial step | POST metadata to `/oauth/register` | Host metadata at a reachable URL |
| Server state | Registration record lives in the token store | Resolver cache plus resolved client record |
| Extra security work | Registration input validation | SSRF protection, fetch limits, URL validation |
| Best fit | Test harnesses and ad hoc clients | Self-describing clients and metadata-driven demos |
| Local dev | Simple | Requires development-mode localhost exceptions |

## Example usage

Run the server first:

```bash
uv sync --dev
uv run uvicorn mcp_auth_test_server.app:app --port 8765
```

Run the DCR example:

```bash
uv run python examples/dcr_client_flow.py --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765
```

Run the CIMD example:

```bash
uv run python examples/cimd_client_flow.py --base-url http://127.0.0.1:8765
```

## What to inspect

- `GET /debug/traces` for structured DCR and CIMD trace events
- `GET /debug/clients` to confirm registered and resolved client records
- `GET /debug/authorizations` and `GET /debug/tokens` to inspect the issued flow state

